Project's Abstract:
Digital identity verification usually requires users to share complete identity documents even when only one fact is
needed. For example, to prove that a person is above 18, their full date of birth, address, photo and other personal
details may be shared.
AegisID is a privacy-preserving digital identity verification system based on Zero-Knowledge Proofs (ZKP) and
blockchain. It allows users to prove a required fact without sharing the complete identity document.
The Zero-Knowledge Proof is created directly on the user's device. The user's private information and private keys stay
on the device and are not sent to the verifier. The system uses technologies such as zk-SNARK, Groth16, Poseidon, Circom
and blockchain smart contracts.
AegisID can be used for bank account opening, credit checks, hotel or SIM verification, tax and compliance checks, and
government benefit services.
The expected result is a faster and more privacy-friendly identity verification process where the user shares only the
required proof instead of the complete identity information.

Existing Problems:
Users often have to share their complete identity documents even when only one piece of information is required.
This can expose private details such as date of birth, address, photo and ID number.
Storing large amounts of identity information in one central database creates a major security risk.
Traditional Video-KYC can take a long time and may require users to wait for a verification agent.
Repeated identity verification creates extra time, cost and effort for both users and companies.

Need:
Verify a person's required information without sharing the complete identity document.
Reduce the amount of personal information shared during verification.
Make identity verification faster and more secure.

Target:
Citizens
Banks and FinTech companies
DeFi and blockchain platforms
Government services

Scope:
AegisID focuses on privacy-preserving identity verification using Zero-Knowledge Proofs and blockchain technology.

Objectives:
To develop a privacy-preserving digital identity verification system using Zero-Knowledge Proofs.
To allow users to prove specific information without sharing their complete identity document.
To create Zero-Knowledge Proofs directly on the user's device.
To keep the user's private keys and personal information on the user's device.
To use blockchain and smart contracts for secure proof verification.
To make identity verification faster, simpler and more secure.
To support identity and compliance checks without unnecessary sharing of personal information.

